<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<usx version="3.1">
<book code="ROM" style="id">BLB-Draft</book>

<para style="h">Romans</para>

<para style="toc1">Romans</para>

<para style="rem">sourceUrl=https://literalbible.com/blb.docx fetchedAtUtc=2026-09-01T15:43:00Z status=draft id=BLB-Draft spec=USFM-USX 3.1.1 char:add=docx-italic note:f=docx-footnotes note:x=docx-heading-crossrefs</para>

<chapter number="1" style="c" sid="ROM 1"/>

<para style="s">Paul Greets the Saints in Rome</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 1:1"/>Paul, a servant of Christ Jesus,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Jesus Christ</char></note> a called apostle having been set apart for the gospel of God, <verse eid="ROM 1:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 1:2"/>which He promised beforehand through His prophets in Holy Scriptures, <verse eid="ROM 1:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 1:3"/>concerning His Son, the One having come of the seed of David according to the flesh, <verse eid="ROM 1:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 1:4"/>the One having been determined the Son of God in power according to the Spirit of holiness by resurrection <char style="add">from</char> the dead: Jesus Christ our Lord, <verse eid="ROM 1:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 1:5"/>through whom we received grace and apostleship for obedience of faith among all the Gentiles on behalf of His name, <verse eid="ROM 1:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 1:6"/>among whom are you⁺ also, called of Jesus Christ. <verse eid="ROM 1:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 1:7"/>To all those being in Rome beloved of God, called saints: </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 1:7">Grace to you⁺ and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. <verse eid="ROM 1:7"/></para>

<para style="s">Unashamed of the Gospel</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 1:8"/>First indeed, I thank my God through Jesus Christ for all of you⁺, because your⁺ faith is being proclaimed in all the world. <verse eid="ROM 1:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 1:9"/>For God, whom I serve in my spirit<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with my spirit</char></note> in the gospel of His Son, is my witness how unceasingly I make remembrance of you⁺, <verse eid="ROM 1:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 1:10"/>always upon my prayers, imploring if somehow now at last by the will of God I will make a prosperous journey to come to you⁺. <verse eid="ROM 1:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 1:11"/>For I long to see you⁺ so that I may impart some spiritual gift to you⁺, to your⁺ being strengthened, <verse eid="ROM 1:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 1:12"/>and, that is, to be encouraged together among you⁺ through the faith among one another, both of you⁺ and of me. <verse eid="ROM 1:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 1:13"/>Now I do not want you⁺ to be ignorant, brothers, that many times I purposed to come to you⁺, and was prevented until the present, so that I might have some fruit among you⁺ also, even as among the other Gentiles. <verse eid="ROM 1:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 1:14"/>I am a debtor both to Greeks and to barbarians,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to Greeks and non-Greeks</char></note> both to wise and to foolish. <verse eid="ROM 1:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 1:15"/>Thus, as to me, <char style="add">I am</char> ready to preach the gospel also to you⁺ who <char style="add">are</char> in Rome. <verse eid="ROM 1:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 1:16"/>For I am not ashamed of the gospel,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Christ</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> for it is the power of God unto salvation to everyone believing—both to Jew first, and to Greek. <verse eid="ROM 1:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 1:17"/>For in it the righteousness of God is revealed from faith to faith, just as it has been written: “And the righteous one will live by faith.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For the gospel reveals the righteousness of God that comes by faith from start to finish</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Habakkuk 2:4</char></note><verse eid="ROM 1:17"/></para>

<para style="s">God’s Wrath against Sin</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 1:18"/>For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men, suppressing the truth in unrighteousness, <verse eid="ROM 1:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 1:19"/>because that which <char style="add">is</char> known of God is manifest among them, for God has revealed <char style="add">it</char> to them. <verse eid="ROM 1:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 1:20"/>For from the creation of the world His invisible <char style="add">qualities</char>, both His eternal power and divinity, are clearly seen, being understood by the things made, for them to be without excuse. <verse eid="ROM 1:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 1:21"/>For having known God, not as God did they glorify <char style="add">Him</char>, or give thanks, but they became futile in their thoughts and their foolish heart was darkened. <verse eid="ROM 1:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 1:22"/>Professing to be wise, they became fools, <verse eid="ROM 1:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 1:23"/>and they changed<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 </char><char style="fq" closed="false">exchanged for themselves</char></note> the glory of the immortal God into the likeness of an image of mortal man and birds and quadrupeds and creeping things. <verse eid="ROM 1:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 1:24"/>Therefore God gave them up in the sinful desires of their hearts to uncleanness to dishonor their bodies among themselves, <verse eid="ROM 1:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 1:25"/>who changed the truth of God into falsehood, and worshiped and served the creature rather than the One having created, who is blessed to the ages!<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">forever worthy of praise</char></note> Amen. <verse eid="ROM 1:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 1:26"/>Because of this, God gave up them to passions of dishonor. For even their females changed the natural use into that contrary to nature. <verse eid="ROM 1:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 1:27"/>And likewise also the males, having left the natural use of the female, were inflamed in their lust toward one another, males with males, working out shame and receiving back in themselves the recompense that was fitting of their error. <verse eid="ROM 1:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 1:28"/>And just as they did not see fit to have God in <char style="add">their</char> knowledge, God gave them up to an unfit mind, to do the <char style="add">things</char> not being proper; <verse eid="ROM 1:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 1:29"/>being filled with all unrighteousness,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">sexual immorality</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> wickedness, covetousness, malice; full of envy, murder, strife, deceit, malice; gossips, <verse eid="ROM 1:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="ROM 1:30"/>slanderers, hateful to God, insolent, arrogant, boasters; inventors of evil, disobedient to parents; <verse eid="ROM 1:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="ROM 1:31"/>unintelligent, untrustworthy, unloving,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">unforgiving</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> unmerciful; <verse eid="ROM 1:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="ROM 1:32"/>who, having known the righteous decree of God that those practicing such things are worthy of death, not only are doing them, but also agree with those practicing <char style="add">them</char>. <verse eid="ROM 1:32"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 1"/>

<chapter number="2" style="c" sid="ROM 2"/>

<para style="s">God’s Righteous Judgment</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 2:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Psalm 75:1–10</char></note>Therefore you are without excuse, O man, every one who is judging. For in that which you judge the other, you are condemning yourself, for you, the one judging, practice the same things. <verse eid="ROM 2:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 2:2"/>But we know that the judgment of God upon those practicing such things is according to truth. <verse eid="ROM 2:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 2:3"/>Now do you reckon this, O man, the one judging those practicing such things, and doing them <char style="add">yourself</char>, that you will escape the judgment of God? <verse eid="ROM 2:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 2:4"/>Or do you despise the riches of His kindness and forbearance and patience, not knowing that the kindness of God leads you to repentance? <verse eid="ROM 2:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 2:5"/>But according to your hardness and unrepentant heart, you are storing up for yourself wrath in the day of wrath and revelation of the righteous judgment of God,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">you are storing up for yourself wrath for the day of wrath, revelation, and the righteous judgment of God,</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 2:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 2:6"/>who “will repay to each according to his works,” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 62:12</char></note><verse eid="ROM 2:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 2:7"/>to those who indeed with perseverance in good work are seeking glory and honor and immortality, eternal life; <verse eid="ROM 2:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 2:8"/>but to those of self-interest and disobeying the truth but obeying unrighteousness, wrath and anger;<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">anger and wrath</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 2:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 2:9"/>tribulation and distress upon every soul of man working evil, both of Jew first, and also of Greek; <verse eid="ROM 2:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 2:10"/>but glory and honor and peace to everyone working good, both to Jew first, and to Greek. <verse eid="ROM 2:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 2:11"/>For there is no partiality with God. <verse eid="ROM 2:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 2:12"/>For as many as have sinned without the law will also perish without the law; and as many as have sinned in the law will be judged by the law, <verse eid="ROM 2:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 2:13"/>for not the hearers of the law <char style="add">are</char> righteous before God; but the doers of the law will be declared righteous. <verse eid="ROM 2:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 2:14"/>For when Gentiles, not having the law, may do by nature the things of the law, these not having a law are a law to themselves, <verse eid="ROM 2:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 2:15"/>who show the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also testifying, and the thoughts between one another accusing or also defending <char style="add">them</char> <verse eid="ROM 2:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 2:16"/>on the day when God judges the secrets of men according to my gospel through Christ Jesus.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">through Jesus Christ</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 2:16"/></para>

<para style="s">The Jews and the Law</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 2:17"/>But if you are named<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Behold, you are named</char></note> a Jew and rest upon the law and boast in God, <verse eid="ROM 2:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 2:18"/>and you know the will and approve the <char style="add">things</char> being superior, being instructed out of the law, <verse eid="ROM 2:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 2:19"/>and you have persuaded yourself <char style="add">that you</char> are a guide of the blind, a light to those in darkness, <verse eid="ROM 2:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 2:20"/>an instructor of the foolish, a teacher of infants, having in the law the form of knowledge and of the truth— <verse eid="ROM 2:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 2:21"/>then <char style="add">you</char> who are teaching another, do you not teach yourself? <char style="add">You</char> who are preaching not to steal, do you steal? <verse eid="ROM 2:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 2:22"/><char style="add">You</char> who are saying not to commit adultery, do you commit adultery? <char style="add">You</char> who are abhorring idols, do you rob temples? <verse eid="ROM 2:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 2:23"/><char style="add">You</char> who boast in the law, do you dishonor God through the transgression of the law? <verse eid="ROM 2:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 2:24"/>As it has been written: “For the name of God is blasphemed among the Gentiles because of you⁺.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 52:5 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 2:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 2:25"/>For circumcision indeed profits if you practice the law; but if you are a transgressor of the law, your circumcision has become uncircumcision. <verse eid="ROM 2:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 2:26"/>Therefore if the uncircumcision keeps the righteous requirements of the law, will not his uncircumcision be reckoned as circumcision? <verse eid="ROM 2:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 2:27"/>And the uncircumcision by nature, fulfilling the law, will judge you who through the letter and circumcision <char style="add">are</char> a transgressor of the law. <verse eid="ROM 2:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 2:28"/>For <char style="add">it is</char> not the one <char style="add">who</char> on the outside is a Jew, nor <char style="add">is</char> <char style="add">that</char> on the outside in flesh circumcision. <verse eid="ROM 2:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 2:29"/>But a Jew <char style="add">is one</char> on the inside, and circumcision <char style="add">is</char> of heart, in spirit, not in letter, of whom the praise <char style="add">is</char> not of men, but of God. <verse eid="ROM 2:29"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 2"/>

<chapter number="3" style="c" sid="ROM 3"/>

<para style="s">God Remains Faithful</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 3:1"/>What then <char style="add">is</char> the superiority of the Jew? Or what <char style="add">is</char> the profit of the circumcision? <verse eid="ROM 3:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 3:2"/>Much in every way. For first indeed, that they were entrusted with the oracles<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the very words</char></note> of God. <verse eid="ROM 3:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 3:3"/>For what If some disbelieved? Will their unbelief nullify the faithfulness of God? <verse eid="ROM 3:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 3:4"/>Never may it be! But let God be true and every man a liar, as it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 3:4">“So that You may be justified in Your words, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:4">and you will be victorious in Your judging.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when You are judged</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Psalm 51:4 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 3:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 3:5"/>But if our unrighteousness proves God’s righteousness, what will we say? God, inflicting the wrath, <char style="add">is</char> unrighteous? I speak according to man. <verse eid="ROM 3:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 3:6"/>Never may it be! Otherwise, how will God judge the world? <verse eid="ROM 3:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 3:7"/>But<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For</char></note> if the truth of God in my falsehood abounded to His glory, why am I also still judged as a sinner? <verse eid="ROM 3:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 3:8"/>And <char style="add">is it</char> not, as we are slandered and as some affirm us to say, “Let us do evil so that the good may come”? Their judgment is just. <verse eid="ROM 3:8"/></para>

<para style="s">There Is No One RighteousPsalm 14:1–7</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 3:9"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Isaiah 59:1–17</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Psalm 53:1–6</char></note>What then? Are we better? Not at all. For we charged before both Jews and Greeks with being all under sin. <verse eid="ROM 3:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 3:10"/>As it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 3:10">“There is no one righteous, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:10">not even one; <verse eid="ROM 3:10"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 3:11"/>there is no one understanding; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:11">there is no one seeking out God. <verse eid="ROM 3:11"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 3:12"/>All turned away; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:12">together they became worthless; </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 3:12">there is none who is doing kindness, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:12">there is not even one.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 14:1–3 and Psalm 53:1–3 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 3:12"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 3:13"/>“Their throat <char style="add">is</char> a grave having been opened; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:13">they keep practicing deceit with their tongues;” </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:13">“the poison of vipers <char style="add">is</char> under their lips,”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 5:9</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 140:3</char></note><verse eid="ROM 3:13"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 3:14"/>“of whom the mouth is full </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:14">of cursing and of bitterness;” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 10:7 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 3:14"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 3:15"/>“their feet <char style="add">are</char> swift to shed blood; <verse eid="ROM 3:15"/></para>

<para style="q2"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 3:16"/>ruin and misery <char style="add">are</char> in their paths; <verse eid="ROM 3:16"/></para>

<para style="q2"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 3:17"/>and a way of peace they did not know.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 59:7–8 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 3:17"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 3:18"/>“There is no fear of God </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 3:18">before their eyes.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 36:1</char></note><verse eid="ROM 3:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 3:19"/>Now we know that as much as the law says, it speaks to those in the law, so that every mouth may be shut, and all the world may be under judgment to God. <verse eid="ROM 3:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 3:20"/>Therefore by works of the law, not any flesh will be justified before Him; for through the law <char style="add">is</char> knowledge of sin. <verse eid="ROM 3:20"/></para>

<para style="s">Righteousness through Faith in ChristPhilippians 3:1–11</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 3:21"/>But now, apart from the law, the righteousness of God has been revealed, being testified to by the Law and the Prophets. <verse eid="ROM 3:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 3:22"/>And the righteousness of God <char style="add">is</char> through faith from Jesus Christ toward all<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and upon all</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> those believing. For there is no distinction, <verse eid="ROM 3:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 3:23"/>for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, <verse eid="ROM 3:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 3:24"/>being justified freely by His grace through the redemption that <char style="add">is</char> in Christ Jesus, <verse eid="ROM 3:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 3:25"/>whom God set forth as a propitiation<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">as an atoning sacrifice</char></note> through faith in His blood, for a demonstration of His righteousness, because of the passing over of the sins having taken place beforehand in the forbearance of God, <verse eid="ROM 3:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 3:26"/>for the demonstration of His righteousness in the present time, for Him to be just and justifying the one of the faith from Jesus. <verse eid="ROM 3:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 3:27"/>Where then <char style="add">is</char> the boasting? It was excluded. By what kind of law? That of works? No, but by the law of faith. <verse eid="ROM 3:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 3:28"/>For<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore</char></note> we reckon a man to be justified by faith apart from works of law. <verse eid="ROM 3:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 3:29"/>Or <char style="add">is He</char> the God of Jews only, not also of Gentiles? Yes, also of Gentiles, <verse eid="ROM 3:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="ROM 3:30"/>since indeed God <char style="add">is</char> one, who will justify the circumcision by faith and the uncircumcision through the <char style="add">same</char> faith. <verse eid="ROM 3:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="ROM 3:31"/>Do we, then, nullify the law through faith? Never may it be! Rather, we uphold the law. <verse eid="ROM 3:31"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 3"/>

<chapter number="4" style="c" sid="ROM 4"/>

<para style="s">Abraham Justified by Faith</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 4:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Hebrews 11:8–19</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Psalm 32:1–11</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Genesis 15:1–7</char></note>What then will we say <char style="add">that</char> Abraham, our forefather according to the flesh, has found? <verse eid="ROM 4:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 4:2"/>For if Abraham was justified by works, he has a ground of boasting, but not before God. <verse eid="ROM 4:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 4:3"/>For what does the Scripture say? “And Abraham believed God, and it was reckoned to him as righteousness.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 15:6</char></note><verse eid="ROM 4:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 4:4"/>Now to the one working, the wage is not reckoned according to grace, but according to debt. <verse eid="ROM 4:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 4:5"/>But to the one not working but believing on the One justifying the ungodly, his faith is reckoned as righteousness, <verse eid="ROM 4:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 4:6"/>just as David also declares the blessedness of the man to whom God reckons righteousness apart from works: <verse eid="ROM 4:6"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 4:7"/>“Blessed <char style="add">are</char> <char style="add">those</char> whose lawless deeds are forgiven, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 4:7">and whose sins are covered; <verse eid="ROM 4:7"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 4:8"/>blessed <char style="add">is</char> the man </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 4:8">to whom the Lord shall never reckon sin.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 32:1–2 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 4:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 4:9"/><char style="add">Is</char> this blessedness, then, on the circumcision, or also on the uncircumcision? For we are saying faith was reckoned to Abraham as righteousness. <verse eid="ROM 4:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 4:10"/>How then was it reckoned—being in circumcision, or in uncircumcision? Not in circumcision, but in uncircumcision.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">In what context was it credited? Was it after his circumcision, or before? It was not after, but before.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 4:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 4:11"/>And he received the sign of circumcision, a seal of the righteousness of the faith that <char style="add">he had while</char> in the uncircumcision, for him to be father of all those believing through uncircumcision, for the righteousness to be reckoned also<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL, TH, and WH do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">also</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> to them, <verse eid="ROM 4:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 4:12"/>and father of circumcision to those not only of circumcision, but also to those walking in the footsteps of the faith of our father Abraham in uncircumcision. <verse eid="ROM 4:12"/></para>

<para style="s">Abraham Receives the PromiseGenesis 15:8–21</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 4:13"/>For not through law <char style="add">was</char> the promise to Abraham or his seed <char style="add">for</char> him to be heir <char style="add">of</char> the world, but through the righteousness of faith. <verse eid="ROM 4:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 4:14"/>For if those of the law <char style="add">are</char> heirs, faith has been made void and the promise has been nullified. <verse eid="ROM 4:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 4:15"/>For the law brings about wrath; and<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for</char></note> where there is no law, neither <char style="add">is there</char> transgression. <verse eid="ROM 4:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 4:16"/>Because of this <char style="add">it is</char> of faith, so that <char style="add">it may be</char> according to grace for the promise to be sure to all the seed, not only to that of the law, but also to that of the faith of Abraham, who is the father of us all <verse eid="ROM 4:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 4:17"/>(as it has been written: “A father of many nations I have set you”) before <char style="add">Him</char> whom he believed—God, the One making alive the dead and calling existing the <char style="add">things</char> not as existing— <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 17:5</char></note><verse eid="ROM 4:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 4:18"/>who against hope in hope believed, for him to become the father of many nations, according to that having been spoken, “So will your seed be.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 15:5</char></note><verse eid="ROM 4:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 4:19"/>And not having become weak in the faith,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">neither</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> he considered his body already having become dead, being about a hundred years old, and the deadness of Sarah’s womb. <verse eid="ROM 4:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 4:20"/>Yet he did not doubt in unbelief in the promise of God, but was empowered in faith, having given glory to God, <verse eid="ROM 4:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 4:21"/>and having been fully assured that what He has promised, He is also able to do. <verse eid="ROM 4:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 4:22"/>Therefore also “it was reckoned to him as righteousness.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 15:6</char></note><verse eid="ROM 4:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 4:23"/>Now it was not written because of him alone that “it was reckoned to him,” <verse eid="ROM 4:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 4:24"/>but also because of us, to whom it is about to be reckoned, to those believing on the One having raised Jesus our Lord out from the dead, <verse eid="ROM 4:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 4:25"/>who was delivered over because of our trespasses, and was raised because of our justification. <verse eid="ROM 4:25"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 4"/>

<chapter number="5" style="c" sid="ROM 5"/>

<para style="s">The Triumph of Faith</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 5:1"/>Therefore, having been justified by faith, we have<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">let us have</char></note> peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ, <verse eid="ROM 5:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 5:2"/>through whom also we have access by faith into this grace in which we stand; and we boast<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">exult</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verses 3 and 11</char></note> in hope of the glory of God. <verse eid="ROM 5:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 5:3"/>And not only <char style="add">so</char>, but also we boast in tribulations, knowing that tribulation produces perseverance; <verse eid="ROM 5:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 5:4"/>and perseverance, character; and character, hope. <verse eid="ROM 5:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 5:5"/>And hope does not put <char style="add">us</char> to shame, because the love of God has been poured out in our hearts through the Holy Spirit, the One having been given to us. <verse eid="ROM 5:5"/></para>

<para style="s">Christ’s Sacrifice for the UngodlyJohn 3:1–21</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 5:6"/>Still indeed Christ, <char style="add">in</char> our still being weak, according to the right time, died for the ungodly. <verse eid="ROM 5:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 5:7"/>For hardly anyone will die for a righteous <char style="add">man</char>, though for the good <char style="add">man</char> perhaps someone would even dare to die. <verse eid="ROM 5:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 5:8"/>But God demonstrates His own love to us, that <char style="add">in</char> our being still sinners, Christ died for us. <verse eid="ROM 5:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 5:9"/>Therefore much more, having been justified now by His blood, we will be saved by Him from wrath! <verse eid="ROM 5:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 5:10"/>For if, being enemies, we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son, much more, having been reconciled, we will be saved in His life! <verse eid="ROM 5:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 5:11"/>And not only <char style="add">so</char>, but also we are boasting in God through our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom we now received reconciliation. <verse eid="ROM 5:11"/></para>

<para style="s">Death in Adam, Life in ChristGenesis 3:1–7</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 5:12"/>Because of this, just as through one man sin entered into the world, and death through sin, so also death passed through to all men, because all sinned. <verse eid="ROM 5:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 5:13"/>For until the law, sin was in the world; but sin is not imputed, there being no law. <verse eid="ROM 5:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 5:14"/>Nevertheless, death reigned from Adam until Moses, even over those not having sinned in the likeness of the transgression of Adam, who is a type of the coming One. <verse eid="ROM 5:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 5:15"/>But not as the trespass, so also <char style="add">is</char> the gracious gift; for if by the trespass of the one the many died, much more the grace of God and the gift in grace, that of the one man, Jesus Christ, abounded to the many! <verse eid="ROM 5:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 5:16"/>And not as through one having sinned <char style="add">is</char> the gift; for indeed the judgment out of one <char style="add">is</char> to condemnation; but the gracious gift out of many trespasses <char style="add">is</char> to justification. <verse eid="ROM 5:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 5:17"/>For if, by the trespass of the one, death reigned through the one, much more will those receiving the abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness reign in life through the one, Jesus Christ! <verse eid="ROM 5:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 5:18"/>So then, just as through one trespass <char style="add">is</char> condemnation to all men so also through one righteous act <char style="add">is</char> justification of life to all men. <verse eid="ROM 5:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 5:19"/>For just as through the disobedience of the one man the many were made sinners, so also through the obedience of the One the many will be made righteous. <verse eid="ROM 5:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 5:20"/>And the law entered so that the trespass might abound; but where sin abounded, grace overabounded, <verse eid="ROM 5:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 5:21"/>so that, just as sin reigned in death, so also grace might reign through righteousness to eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord. <verse eid="ROM 5:21"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 5"/>

<chapter number="6" style="c" sid="ROM 6"/>

<para style="s">Dead to Sin, Alive to God</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 6:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">2 Corinthians 4:7–18</char></note>What then will we say? Shall we continue in sin so that grace may abound? <verse eid="ROM 6:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 6:2"/>Never may it be! <char style="add">We</char> who died to sin, how shall we still live in it? <verse eid="ROM 6:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 6:3"/>Or are you⁺ ignorant that as many as were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into His death? <verse eid="ROM 6:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 6:4"/>We were buried, therefore, with Him through baptism into death, so that, just as Christ was raised out from the dead through the glory of the Father, so we also should walk in newness of life. <verse eid="ROM 6:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 6:5"/>For if we have become united in the likeness of His death, yet also we will be of the resurrection, <verse eid="ROM 6:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 6:6"/>knowing this, that our old man was crucified with <char style="add">Him</char>, so that the body of sin might be nullified, <char style="add">for</char> us no longer to be slaves to sin. <verse eid="ROM 6:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 6:7"/>For the one having died has been justified from sin. <verse eid="ROM 6:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 6:8"/>Now if we died with Christ, we believe that we will also live with Him, <verse eid="ROM 6:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 6:9"/>knowing that Christ, having been raised out from the dead, dies no more; death no longer rules over Him. <verse eid="ROM 6:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 6:10"/>For that which He died, He died to sin once for all; but that which He lives, He lives to God. <verse eid="ROM 6:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 6:11"/>So also you⁺, reckon yourselves to be dead indeed to sin, but living to God in Christ Jesus. <verse eid="ROM 6:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 6:12"/>Therefore do not let sin reign in your⁺ mortal body, in order to obey its desires. <verse eid="ROM 6:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 6:13"/>Neither present your⁺ members to sin <char style="add">as</char> instruments of unrighteousness, but present yourselves to God as living out from the dead, and your⁺ members to God <char style="add">as</char> instruments of righteousness. <verse eid="ROM 6:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 6:14"/>For sin will not rule over you⁺, for you⁺ are not under law, but under grace. <verse eid="ROM 6:14"/></para>

<para style="s">The Wages of Sin</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 6:15"/>What then? Shall we sin because we are not under law, but under grace? Never may it be! <verse eid="ROM 6:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 6:16"/>Do you⁺ not know that to whom you⁺ present yourselves <char style="add">as</char> slaves for obedience, you⁺ are slaves to the one whom you⁺ obey, whether of sin unto death, or of obedience unto righteousness? <verse eid="ROM 6:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 6:17"/>But thanks <char style="add">be</char> to God that you⁺ used to be slaves of sin, but you⁺ obeyed from the heart the form of teaching to which you⁺ were delivered up. <verse eid="ROM 6:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 6:18"/>Now having been set free from sin, you⁺ became slaves to righteousness. <verse eid="ROM 6:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 6:19"/>I speak in human terms because of the weakness of your⁺ flesh. For just as you⁺ presented your⁺ members <char style="add">as</char> slaves to uncleanness and to lawlessness unto lawlessness, so now present your⁺ members <char style="add">as</char> slaves to righteousness unto sanctification. <verse eid="ROM 6:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 6:20"/>For when you⁺ were slaves of sin, you⁺ were free from righteousness. <verse eid="ROM 6:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 6:21"/>What fruit, therefore, were you⁺ having then in the <char style="add">things</char> of which you⁺ are now ashamed? For the end of those <char style="add">things</char> <char style="add">is</char> death. <verse eid="ROM 6:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 6:22"/>But now, having been set free from sin and having become slaves to God, you⁺ have your⁺ fruit unto sanctification, and the end <char style="add">is</char> eternal life. <verse eid="ROM 6:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 6:23"/>For the wages of sin <char style="add">is</char> death, but the gift of God <char style="add">is</char> eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 6:23"> BYZ and TR include <char style="add">our Lord</char>. BYZ and TR <char style="add">to obey it in its desires</char> See Proverbs 10:16.<verse eid="ROM 6:23"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 6"/>

<chapter number="7" style="c" sid="ROM 7"/>

<para style="s">Release from the Law</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 7:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Galatians 3:15–25</char></note>Or are you⁺ ignorant, brothers (for I speak to those knowing the law), that the law rules over the man for as long as the time he lives? <verse eid="ROM 7:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 7:2"/>For the married woman is bound by law to the living husband; but if the husband should die, she is released from the law of the husband. <verse eid="ROM 7:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 7:3"/>So then, of the husband being alive, if she becomes unto another man, she will be called an adulteress; but if the husband should die, she is free from the law, <char style="add">so as</char> not for her to be an adulteress, having become unto another man. <verse eid="ROM 7:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 7:4"/>Likewise, my brothers, you⁺ also have been put to death to the law through the body of Christ, for you⁺ to become unto another, unto the One having been raised out from the dead, so that we should bear fruit to God. <verse eid="ROM 7:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 7:5"/>For when we were in the flesh, the passions of sins that <char style="add">were</char> through the law were working in our members, to the bearing of fruit to death. <verse eid="ROM 7:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 7:6"/>But now we have been released from the law, having died to that by which we were held, so as for us to serve in the newness of the Spirit, and not in the oldness of the letter. <verse eid="ROM 7:6"/></para>

<para style="s">God’s Law Is Holy</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 7:7"/>What then will we say? <char style="add">Is</char> the law sin? Never may it be! But I did not know sin, if not through the law. For I would not have been conscious of covetousness if the law had not said, “You shall not covet.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 20:17; Deuteronomy 5:21</char></note><verse eid="ROM 7:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 7:8"/>But sin, having taken the opportunity through the commandment, produced in me all covetousness; for apart from the law, sin <char style="add">is</char> dead. <verse eid="ROM 7:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 7:9"/>And I was once alive apart from the law, but <char style="add">on</char> the commandment's having come, sin revived and I died; <verse eid="ROM 7:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 7:10"/>and the commandment that <char style="add">was</char> unto life, this was found by me unto death. <verse eid="ROM 7:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 7:11"/>For sin, having taken the opportunity through the commandment, deceived me and through it killed <char style="add">me</char>. <verse eid="ROM 7:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 7:12"/>So indeed, the law <char style="add">is</char> holy, and the commandment <char style="add">is</char> holy and righteous and good. <verse eid="ROM 7:12"/></para>

<para style="s">Struggling with Sin</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 7:13"/>Did that which<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Has that which</char></note> <char style="add">is</char> good, then, become death to me? Never may it be! But so that sin might be shown as sin, it is producing death through that which <char style="add">is</char> good to me, so that through the commandment sin might become sinful beyond excess. <verse eid="ROM 7:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 7:14"/>For we have known that the law is spiritual; but I am fleshly, having been sold as a slave under sin. <verse eid="ROM 7:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 7:15"/>For what I carry out, I do not understand. For what I want, this I do not practice; but what I hate, this I do. <verse eid="ROM 7:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 7:16"/>Now if what I do not want, this I do, I assent to the law, that <char style="add">it is</char> good. <verse eid="ROM 7:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 7:17"/>But now I no longer carry it out, but the sin dwelling in me. <verse eid="ROM 7:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 7:18"/>For I know that there does not dwell in me—that is, in my flesh—good. For to want is present with me, but not<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">but I cannot find how</char></note> to carry out the good. <verse eid="ROM 7:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 7:19"/>For the good that I want, I do not do; but the evil that I do not want, this I practice. <verse eid="ROM 7:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 7:20"/>Now if what I do not want, this I do, no longer do I carry it out, but sin dwelling in me. <verse eid="ROM 7:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 7:21"/>I find, then, the law in my wanting to do good, that evil is present with me. <verse eid="ROM 7:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 7:22"/>For I delight in the law of God according to the inner man, <verse eid="ROM 7:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 7:23"/>but I see another law in my members, warring against the law of my mind and making me captive to the law of sin being in my members.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">captive to the law of sin that dwells within me.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 7:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 7:24"/>O wretched man <char style="add">that</char> I am! Who will deliver me out of this body of death? <verse eid="ROM 7:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 7:25"/>But thanks <char style="add">be</char> to God<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">I thank God</char></note> through Jesus Christ our Lord! </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 7:25">So then, I myself indeed with the mind serve God’s law, but <char style="add">with</char> the flesh the law of sin. <verse eid="ROM 7:25"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 7"/>

<chapter number="8" style="c" sid="ROM 8"/>

<para style="s">Walking by the Spirit</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 8:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Galatians 5:16–26</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Ezekiel 36:16–38</char></note>Therefore <char style="add">there is</char> now no condemnation to those in Christ Jesus.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in Christ Jesus, who do not walk according to the flesh but according to the Spirit.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 8:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 8:2"/>For the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus set you free<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">set me free</char></note> from the law of sin and death. <verse eid="ROM 8:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 8:3"/>For what the law <char style="add">was</char> incapable of in that it was weak through the flesh, God, having sent His Son in the likeness of flesh of sin and for sin,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in the likeness of sinful man, as an offering for sin</char></note> condemned sin in the flesh, <verse eid="ROM 8:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 8:4"/>so that the righteous requirement of the law might be fulfilled in us, those not walking according to the flesh but according to the Spirit. <verse eid="ROM 8:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 8:5"/>For those being according to the flesh mind the <char style="add">things</char> of the flesh, but those according to the Spirit the <char style="add">things</char> of the Spirit. <verse eid="ROM 8:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 8:6"/>For the mind of the flesh <char style="add">is</char> death, but the mind of the Spirit <char style="add">is</char> life and peace, <verse eid="ROM 8:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 8:7"/>because the mind of the flesh <char style="add">is</char> hostility toward God; for it does not subject itself to the law of God, for <char style="add">it is</char> not even able. <verse eid="ROM 8:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 8:8"/>And those being in the flesh<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Those controlled by the flesh</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; similarly in verse 9</char></note> are not able to please God. <verse eid="ROM 8:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 8:9"/>But you⁺ are not in the flesh but in the Spirit, if indeed the Spirit of God dwells in you⁺; and if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, this one is not of Him. <verse eid="ROM 8:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 8:10"/>But if Christ <char style="add">is</char> in you⁺, the body <char style="add">is</char> indeed dead because of sin, but the spirit <char style="add">is</char> life because of righteousness. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Spirit</char></note><verse eid="ROM 8:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 8:11"/>And if the Spirit of the One having raised Jesus out from the dead dwells in you⁺, the One having raised Christ Jesus out from the dead<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NA, BYZ, and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the One having raised Christ out from the dead</char></note> will also make alive your⁺ mortal bodies through His Spirit, the One dwelling in you⁺. <verse eid="ROM 8:11"/></para>

<para style="s">Heirs with Christ</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 8:12"/>So then, brothers, we are debtors, not to the flesh, to live according to the flesh. <verse eid="ROM 8:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 8:13"/>For if you⁺ live according to the flesh, you⁺ are about to die; but if by the Spirit you⁺ put to death the deeds of the body, you⁺ will live. <verse eid="ROM 8:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 8:14"/>For as many as are led by the Spirit of God, these are sons of God. <verse eid="ROM 8:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 8:15"/>For you⁺ did not receive a spirit of slavery again unto fear, but you⁺ received the Spirit of adoption as sons, in whom we cry, “Abba! Father!” <verse eid="ROM 8:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 8:16"/>The Spirit Himself testifies with our spirit that we are children of God, <verse eid="ROM 8:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 8:17"/>and if children, also heirs: heirs indeed of God, and co-heirs of Christ, if indeed we suffer with <char style="add">Him</char>, so that we may also be glorified with <char style="add">Him</char>. <verse eid="ROM 8:17"/></para>

<para style="s">Future Glory2 Corinthians 5:1–10</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 8:18"/>For I reckon that the sufferings of the present time <char style="add">are</char> not comparable to the glory about to be revealed in us. <verse eid="ROM 8:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 8:19"/>For the eager expectation of the creation eagerly awaits the revelation of the sons of God. <verse eid="ROM 8:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 8:20"/>For the creation was subjected to futility, not willingly, but because of the One having subjected <char style="add">it</char>, in hope <verse eid="ROM 8:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 8:21"/>that the creation itself will also be set free from the bondage of decay, into the freedom of the glory of the children of God. <verse eid="ROM 8:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 8:22"/>For we know that all the creation groans together and travails together until now. <verse eid="ROM 8:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 8:23"/>And not only <char style="add">so</char>, but we ourselves, even having the firstfruit of the Spirit, even we ourselves groan within ourselves, eagerly awaiting adoption as sons, the redemption of our body. <verse eid="ROM 8:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 8:24"/>For in hope we were saved, but hope being seen is not hope. For does anyone hope for<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Why does he hope for</char></note> what he sees? <verse eid="ROM 8:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 8:25"/>But if we hope for what we do not see, we eagerly await in perseverance. <verse eid="ROM 8:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 8:26"/>Now likewise also, the Spirit joins to help in our weakness;<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">weaknesses</char></note> for we do not know the <char style="add">things</char> that we should pray for as it is necessary, but the Spirit Himself intercedes<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for us</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> with inexpressible groanings. <verse eid="ROM 8:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 8:27"/>And the One searching hearts knows what <char style="add">is</char> the mind of the Spirit, because He intercedes for the saints according to God. <verse eid="ROM 8:27"/></para>

<para style="s">God Works in All ThingsEphesians 1:3–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 8:28"/>And we know that God works together all things<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">that all things work together</char></note> for good to those loving God, to those being called according to purpose, <verse eid="ROM 8:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 8:29"/>because whom He foreknew, He also predestined <char style="add">to be</char> conformed to the image of His Son, for Him to be firstborn among many brothers. <verse eid="ROM 8:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="ROM 8:30"/>And whom He predestined, these also He called; and whom He called, these also He justified; and whom He justified, these also He glorified. <verse eid="ROM 8:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="ROM 8:31"/>What then will we say to these things? If God <char style="add">is</char> for us, who <char style="add">can be</char> against us— <verse eid="ROM 8:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="ROM 8:32"/>He who indeed did not spare <char style="add">His</char> own Son, but delivered Him up for us all, how will He not also with Him graciously give us all things? <verse eid="ROM 8:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="ROM 8:33"/>Who will bring an accusation against the chosen ones of God? God <char style="add">is</char> the One justifying. <verse eid="ROM 8:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="ROM 8:34"/>Who <char style="add">is</char> the one condemning? Christ Jesus<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Christ</char></note> the One having died, now rather having been raised, <char style="add">is</char> He who is also at the right hand of God, and who is interceding for us. <verse eid="ROM 8:34"/></para>

<para style="s">More than ConquerorsPsalm 44:1–26</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="ROM 8:35"/>Who will separate us from the love of Christ? Will tribulation or distress or persecution or famine or nakedness or danger or sword? <verse eid="ROM 8:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="ROM 8:36"/>As it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 8:36">“For Your sake we face death all the day; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 8:36">we were reckoned as sheep of slaughter.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 44:22</char></note><verse eid="ROM 8:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="ROM 8:37"/>But in all these things we are more than conquerors through the One having loved us. <verse eid="ROM 8:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="ROM 8:38"/>For I am persuaded that neither death nor life, nor angels nor principalities, nor things present nor things about to be, nor powers,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">nor powers, nor things present, nor things about to be</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 8:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="ROM 8:39"/>nor height nor depth, nor any other created thing, will be able to separate us from the love of God that <char style="add">is</char> in Christ Jesus our Lord. <verse eid="ROM 8:39"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 8"/>

<chapter number="9" style="c" sid="ROM 9"/>

<para style="s">Paul’s Concern for the Jews</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 9:1"/>I speak truth in Christ—I am not lying, my conscience is testifying with me in the Holy Spirit <verse eid="ROM 9:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 9:2"/>that sorrow to me is great, and unceasing pain <char style="add">is</char> in my heart. <verse eid="ROM 9:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 9:3"/>For I could wish myself to be accursed, apart from Christ for my brothers, my kinsmen according to the flesh, <verse eid="ROM 9:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 9:4"/>who are Israelites, whose <char style="add">is</char> the adoption as sons and the glory and the covenants and the lawgiving and the service and the promises, <verse eid="ROM 9:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 9:5"/>whose <char style="add">are</char> the fathers, and from whom <char style="add">is</char> Christ according to the flesh, being God over all, blessed to the ages!<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">forever worthy of praise</char></note> Amen. <verse eid="ROM 9:5"/></para>

<para style="s">God’s Sovereign ChoiceGenesis 25:19–28</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 9:6"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Malachi 1:1–5</char></note>But <char style="add">it is</char> not as though the word of God has failed. For not all who <char style="add">are</char> of Israel, <char style="add">are</char> these Israel. <verse eid="ROM 9:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 9:7"/>Nor because they are seed of Abraham <char style="add">are</char> all children. Rather, “In Isaac a seed will be called to you.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 21:12</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 9:8"/>That is, the children of the flesh, these <char style="add">are</char> not children of God; but the children of the promise are reckoned for seed. <verse eid="ROM 9:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 9:9"/>For this <char style="add">is</char> the word of promise: “At this time I will come, and to Sarah there will be a son.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 18:14</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 9:10"/>And not only <char style="add">so</char>, but also Rebecca, having conception by one, Isaac our father— <verse eid="ROM 9:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 9:11"/>for they not yet having been born nor having done anything good or evil, so that the purpose of God according to election might stand— <verse eid="ROM 9:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 9:12"/>not of works but of the One calling, it was said to her, “The greater will serve the lesser.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Genesis 25:23</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 9:13"/>As it has been written: “Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Malachi 1:2–3</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 9:14"/>What then will we say? <char style="add">Is there</char> injustice with God? Never may it be! <verse eid="ROM 9:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 9:15"/>For He says to Moses: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:15">“I will have mercy on whom I may have mercy, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:15">and I will have compassion on whom I may have compassion.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 33:19</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 9:16"/>So then, <char style="add">it is</char> not of the one willing, nor of the one running, but of God having mercy. <verse eid="ROM 9:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 9:17"/>For the Scripture says to Pharaoh: “For this very thing I raised you up, so that I might display My power in you, and that My name should be declared in all the earth.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 9:16 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 9:18"/>So then, He has mercy on whom He wants, and He hardens whom He wants. <verse eid="ROM 9:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 9:19"/>You will say, then, to me, “Why then<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Why</char></note> does He still find fault? For who has resisted His will?” <verse eid="ROM 9:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 9:20"/>But rather, O man, who are you answering against God? <char style="add">Will</char> the thing formed say to the One having formed <char style="add">it</char>, “Why did you make me thus?” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 29:16; Isaiah 45:9</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 9:21"/>Or does the potter not have authority over the clay to make out of the same lump the one vessel indeed for honor, but the one for dishonor? <verse eid="ROM 9:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 9:22"/>And <char style="add">what</char> if God, desiring to display the wrath and to make known His power, bore with much patience vessels of wrath, having been fitted for destruction, <verse eid="ROM 9:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 9:23"/>so that He might also make known the riches of His glory upon vessels of mercy, which He prepared beforehand for glory, <verse eid="ROM 9:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 9:24"/>even us, whom He called not only out from Jews, but also out from Gentiles? <verse eid="ROM 9:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 9:25"/>As He also says in Hosea: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:25">“I will call that which <char style="add">is</char> not My people ‘My People,’ </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:25">and her not beloved ‘Beloved,’” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Hosea 2:23</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 9:26"/>and, </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:26">“It will be <char style="add">that</char> in the place where it was said to them, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:26">‘You⁺ <char style="add">are</char> not My people,’ </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:26">there they will be called </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:26">‘sons of the living God.”” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Hosea 1:10</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 9:27"/>And Isaiah cries out concerning Israel: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:27">“Though the number of the sons of Israel may be like the sand of the sea, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:27"><char style="add">only</char> the remnant will be saved. <verse eid="ROM 9:27"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 9:28"/>For the Lord will execute <char style="add">His</char> word, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:28">finishing and accomplishing swiftly upon the earth.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 10:22–23 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 9:29"/>And, as Isaiah forewarned: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:29">“Unless the Lord of Hosts had left us a seed, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:29">we would have become like Sodom, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:29">and we would have been made like Gomorrah.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Lord of Armies</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Lord of the Saboath</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 1:9 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:29"/></para>

<para style="s">Israel’s Unbelief</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="ROM 9:30"/>What then will we say? That the Gentiles, not pursuing righteousness, obtained righteousness—and righteousness that <char style="add">is</char> by faith; <verse eid="ROM 9:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="ROM 9:31"/>but Israel, pursuing a law of righteousness, did not attain to <char style="add">that</char> law.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">did not attain a law of righteousness</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 9:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="ROM 9:32"/>Why? Because <char style="add">it was</char> not by faith, but as by works. They stumbled<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.32 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">by works of the law. For they stumbled</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> over the stone of stumbling, <verse eid="ROM 9:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="ROM 9:33"/>as it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:33">“Behold, I lay in Zion a stone of stumbling </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:33">and a rock of offense; </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 9:33">and the one believing on Him </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 9:33">will not be put to shame.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 8:14</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 28:16 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 9:33"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 9"/>

<chapter number="10" style="c" sid="ROM 10"/>

<para style="s">The Word Brings Salvation</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 10:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Isaiah 65:1–16</char></note>Brothers, indeed the good pleasure of my heart and <char style="add">my</char> supplication to God on behalf of them <char style="add">is</char> for salvation. <verse eid="ROM 10:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 10:2"/>For I testify about them that they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge. <verse eid="ROM 10:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 10:3"/>For being ignorant of the righteousness of God and seeking to establish <char style="add">their</char> own righteousness,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to establish their own</char></note> they did not submit to the righteousness of God. <verse eid="ROM 10:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 10:4"/>For the end of law <char style="add">is</char> Christ, unto righteousness to everyone believing. <verse eid="ROM 10:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 10:5"/>For Moses writes <char style="add">of</char> the righteousness that <char style="add">is</char> of the law, that, “The man having done these things will live in them.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Leviticus 18:5; see also Ezekiel 20:11, 13, and 21.</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 10:6"/>But the righteousness of faith speaks thus: “You should not say in your heart, ‘Who will ascend into heaven?’ (that is, to bring down Christ), <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 30:12</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 10:7"/>or, ‘Who will descend into the Abyss?’ (that is, to bring up Christ out from the dead).” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Deuteronomy 30:13.</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 10:8"/>But what does it say? “The word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart,” that is, the word of faith that we proclaim, <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 30:14</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 10:9"/>that if you confess<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">confess the word</char></note> with your mouth, “Jesus <char style="add">is</char> Lord,” and believe in your heart that God raised Him out from the dead, you will be saved. <verse eid="ROM 10:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 10:10"/>For with the heart one believes unto righteousness, and with the mouth one confesses unto salvation. <verse eid="ROM 10:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 10:11"/>For the Scripture says, “Everyone believing on Him will not be put to shame.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 28:16 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 10:12"/>For there is no difference between Jew and Greek; for the same Lord of all is rich to all those calling upon Him, <verse eid="ROM 10:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 10:13"/>for, “Whoever shall call upon the name of the Lord will be saved.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Joel 2:32</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 10:14"/>How then can they call upon One in whom they did not believe? And how can they believe in One of whom they did not hear? And how can they hear apart from one preaching? <verse eid="ROM 10:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 10:15"/>And how can they preach unless they can be sent? As it has been written: “How beautiful <char style="add">are</char> the feet of those proclaiming good news of good things!” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">How beautiful are the feet of those proclaiming good news of peace, proclaiming good news of good things</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Isaiah 52:7</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 10:16"/>But not all heeded the good news. For Isaiah says, “Lord, who has believed our report?” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 53:1</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 10:17"/>So faith <char style="add">is</char> by hearing, and hearing <char style="add">is</char> through the word of Christ.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the word of God</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 10:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 10:18"/>But I say, did they not hear? Indeed: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 10:18">“Their voice went out into all the earth, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 10:18">and their words to the ends of the world.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 19:4 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 10:19"/>But I say, did Israel not know? First, Moses says: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 10:19">“I will provoke you⁺ to jealousy by <char style="add">those</char> not a nation; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 10:19">I will provoke you⁺ to anger by a nation without understanding.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 32:21 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 10:20"/>And Isaiah is very bold and says: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 10:20">“I was found by those not seeking Me; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 10:20">I made <char style="add">Myself</char> manifest to those not inquiring after Me.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 65:1</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 10:21"/>But to Israel he says: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 10:21">“All the day I stretched out My hands </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 10:21">to a disobeying and contradicting people.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 65:2 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 10:21"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 10"/>

<chapter number="11" style="c" sid="ROM 11"/>

<para style="s">A Remnant Chosen by Grace</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 11:1"/>I say then, did God thrust away His people? Never may it be! For I also am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin. <verse eid="ROM 11:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 11:2"/>God did not thrust away His people, whom He foreknew. Or do you⁺ not know what the Scripture says in Elijah, how he pleads with God against Israel:<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">saying</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 11:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 11:3"/>“Lord, they killed Your prophets, they tore down Your altars, and I alone was left, and they seek my life”? <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> 1 Kings 19:10, 14</char></note><verse eid="ROM 11:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 11:4"/>But what does the divine answer say to him? “I left to Myself seven thousand men, who did not bow the knee to Baal.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> 1 Kings 19:18</char></note><verse eid="ROM 11:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 11:5"/>So then also in the present time, there has been a remnant according to the election of grace, <verse eid="ROM 11:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 11:6"/>and if by grace, no longer out of works; otherwise grace no longer is grace.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">But if it is by works, then it is no longer grace; otherwise work is no longer work.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 11:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 11:7"/>What then? What Israel seeks after, this it did not obtain, but the elect obtained <char style="add">it</char>. And the rest were hardened, <verse eid="ROM 11:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 11:8"/>as it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 11:8">God gave “them a spirit of stupor, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:8">eyes not to see, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:8">and ears not to hear, </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 11:8">unto this very day.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Deuteronomy 29:4 and Isaiah 29:10.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 11:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 11:9"/>And David says: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 11:9">May their table “become for a snare and for a trap, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:9">and for a stumbling block and for a retribution to them. <verse eid="ROM 11:9"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 11:10"/>May their eyes be darkened not to see, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:10">and their backs bent over through all <char style="add">time</char>.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 69:22–23 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 11:10"/></para>

<para style="s">The Ingrafting of the Gentiles</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 11:11"/>I say then, did they stumble so that they might fall?<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">did they stumble so as to fall beyond recovery?</char></note> Never may it be! But in their trespass <char style="add">is</char> salvation to the Gentiles, so as to provoke them to jealousy. <verse eid="ROM 11:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 11:12"/>But if their trespass <char style="add">is</char> the riches of the world, and their failure <char style="add">is</char> the riches of the Gentiles, how much more their fullness! <verse eid="ROM 11:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 11:13"/>Now I speak to you⁺, the Gentiles. Therefore indeed inasmuch I am an apostle of the Gentiles, I glorify my ministry, <verse eid="ROM 11:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 11:14"/>if somehow I will provoke my <char style="add">own</char> flesh to jealousy and will save some of them. <verse eid="ROM 11:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 11:15"/>For if their rejection <char style="add">is</char> the reconciliation of the world, what <char style="add">will be</char> the acceptance if not life out from the dead? <verse eid="ROM 11:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 11:16"/>Now if the firstfruit <char style="add">is</char> holy, also <char style="add">is</char> the lump; and if the root <char style="add">is</char> holy, also <char style="add">are</char> the branches. <verse eid="ROM 11:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 11:17"/>But if some of the branches were broken off, and you, being a wild olive tree, were grafted in among them and became a fellow partaker of the fatness of the root of the olive tree, <verse eid="ROM 11:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 11:18"/>do not boast over the branches. But if you boast over <char style="add">them</char>, you do not support the root, but the root you. <verse eid="ROM 11:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 11:19"/>You will say, then, “Branches were broken off so that I might be grafted in.” <verse eid="ROM 11:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 11:20"/>Rightly so: They were broken off by unbelief, but you have stood by faith. Do not be high-minded, but be afraid. <verse eid="ROM 11:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 11:21"/>For if God did not spare the natural branches, perhaps He will not spare you.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL, NE, TH, and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">neither will He spare you</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 11:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 11:22"/>Behold, therefore, the kindness and severity of God: severity indeed upon those having fallen, but kindness of God upon you, if you continue in the kindness; otherwise you also will be cut off. <verse eid="ROM 11:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 11:23"/>And even those, if they do not continue in unbelief, will be grafted in, for God is able to graft them in again. <verse eid="ROM 11:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 11:24"/>For if you were cut off out of the naturally wild olive tree, and contrary to nature were grafted into a cultivated olive tree, how much more will these, the natural, be grafted into <char style="add">their</char> own olive tree! <verse eid="ROM 11:24"/></para>

<para style="s">All Israel Will Be Saved</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 11:25"/>For I do not want you⁺ to be ignorant, brothers, of this mystery, so that you⁺ may not be wise in yourselves: A hardening in part has happened to Israel, until the fullness of the Gentiles may come in. <verse eid="ROM 11:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 11:26"/>And thus all Israel will be saved, as it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 11:26">“The Delivering One will come out of Zion; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:26">He will remove ungodliness from Jacob. <verse eid="ROM 11:26"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 11:27"/>And this <char style="add">is</char> the covenant from Me to them, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:27">when I may take away their sins.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 27:9 and Isaiah 59:20–21; see also LXX</char></note><verse eid="ROM 11:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 11:28"/>For regarding the gospel, <char style="add">they are</char> enemies on account of you⁺; but as regards election, beloved on account of the fathers. <verse eid="ROM 11:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 11:29"/>For the gracious gifts and the calling of God <char style="add">are</char> irrevocable. <verse eid="ROM 11:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="ROM 11:30"/>For as you⁺<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">also</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> once disobeyed God, but now received mercy by the disobedience of these, <verse eid="ROM 11:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="ROM 11:31"/>so these also now disobeyed so that through your⁺ mercy they also may now receive mercy.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">they also may receive mercy</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 11:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="ROM 11:32"/>For God closed up all in disobedience so that He may have mercy on all. <verse eid="ROM 11:32"/></para>

<para style="s">A Hymn of PraiseIsaiah 40:9–31</para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="ROM 11:33"/>O, the depth of riches, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:33">both of wisdom and knowledge of God! </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 11:33">How unsearchable His judgments, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:33">and untraceable His ways! <verse eid="ROM 11:33"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="ROM 11:34"/>“For who knew the mind of the Lord, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:34">or who became His counselor?” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 40:13 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 11:34"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="ROM 11:35"/>Or “who first gave to Him, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:35">and it will be given back to him?” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Job 41:11</char></note><verse eid="ROM 11:35"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="ROM 11:36"/>For from Him and through Him and unto Him <char style="add">are</char> all things. </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 11:36">To Him <char style="add">be</char> the glory to the ages! Amen. <verse eid="ROM 11:36"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 11"/>

<chapter number="12" style="c" sid="ROM 12"/>

<para style="s">Living Sacrifices</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 12:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 Corinthians 6:18–20</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 Corinthians 3:16–23</char></note>Therefore I exhort you⁺, brothers, through the compassions of God, to present your⁺ bodies <char style="add">as</char> a living sacrifice, holy to God, well-pleasing, which is your⁺ reasonable service.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">your reasonable service</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 12:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 12:2"/>And do not be conformed to this age, but be transformed by the renewing of the mind, for you⁺ to discern what <char style="add">is</char> the good and well-pleasing and perfect will of God. <verse eid="ROM 12:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 12:3"/>For through the grace having been given to me, I say to everyone being among you⁺ not to be high-minded above what it is necessary to think, but to think so as to be sound-minded, as God allotted to each a measure of faith. <verse eid="ROM 12:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 12:4"/>For just as in one body we have many members, and not all the members have the same function, <verse eid="ROM 12:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 12:5"/>so we, the many, are one body in Christ, and individually members one of another. <verse eid="ROM 12:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 12:6"/>And we are having different gifts according to the grace having been given to us: if prophecy, according to the proportion of faith; <verse eid="ROM 12:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 12:7"/>and if service, in the service; and if teaching, in the teaching; <verse eid="ROM 12:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 12:8"/>and if exhorting, in the exhortation; giving, in generosity; leading, in diligence; showing mercy, in cheerfulness. <verse eid="ROM 12:8"/></para>

<para style="s">Love, Zeal, Hope, HospitalityJohn 13:31–35</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 12:9"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 John 3:11–24</char></note><char style="add">Let</char> love <char style="add">be</char> unfeigned: abhorring evil, cleaving to good, <verse eid="ROM 12:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 12:10"/>devoted to one another in brotherly love, esteeming one another in honor, <verse eid="ROM 12:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 12:11"/>not slothful in diligence, being fervent in spirit, serving the Lord, <verse eid="ROM 12:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 12:12"/>rejoicing in hope, enduring in tribulation, persisting in prayer, <verse eid="ROM 12:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 12:13"/>contributing to the needs of the saints, pursuing hospitality. <verse eid="ROM 12:13"/></para>

<para style="s">ForgivenessMatthew 18:21–35</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 12:14"/>Bless those persecuting you⁺; bless and do not curse, <verse eid="ROM 12:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 12:15"/>to rejoice with the rejoicing, to weep with the weeping, <verse eid="ROM 12:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 12:16"/>minding the same thing toward one another, not minding the high <char style="add">things</char>, but going along with the lowly. Do not be wise in yourselves, <verse eid="ROM 12:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 12:17"/>repaying to no one evil for evil, giving forethought to right <char style="add">things</char> before all men; <verse eid="ROM 12:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 12:18"/>if possible of you⁺, being at peace with all men; <verse eid="ROM 12:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 12:19"/>not avenging yourselves, beloved; but give place to wrath, for it has been written: “Vengeance <char style="add">is</char> Mine, I will repay, says the Lord.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 32:35 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 12:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 12:20"/>Rather, </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 12:20">“If your enemy should hunger, feed him; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 12:20">if he should thirst, give him a drink; </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 12:20">for doing this, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 12:20">you will heap coals of fire upon his head.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Proverbs 25:21–22 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 12:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 12:21"/>Do not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil in good. <verse eid="ROM 12:21"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 12"/>

<chapter number="13" style="c" sid="ROM 13"/>

<para style="s">Submission to Authorities</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 13:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 Peter 2:13–20</char></note>Let every soul be subject to the authorities being above <char style="add">him</char>. For there is no authority except by God; but those existing are appointed by God. <verse eid="ROM 13:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 13:2"/>Therefore the one setting <char style="add">himself</char> against the authority has resisted the ordinance of God, and those having resisted will receive judgment to themselves. <verse eid="ROM 13:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 13:3"/>For rulers are not a terror to good works, but to evil. Now do you want not to fear the authority? Do that which <char style="add">is</char> good, and you will have praise from him. <verse eid="ROM 13:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 13:4"/>For he is God’s servant to you for good. But if you do evil, be afraid, for he does not bear the sword in vain. For he is God’s servant, an avenger for wrath to the one practicing evil. <verse eid="ROM 13:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 13:5"/>Therefore <char style="add">it is</char> necessary to be subject, not only because of wrath, but also because of the conscience. <verse eid="ROM 13:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 13:6"/>For because of this, you⁺ also pay taxes; for they are ministers of God, attending continually to this very thing. <verse eid="ROM 13:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 13:7"/>Pay<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore pay</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> to all their dues: tax to whom tax, revenue to whom revenue, fear to whom fear, honor to whom honor. <verse eid="ROM 13:7"/></para>

<para style="s">Love Fulfills the LawLeviticus 19:9–18</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 13:8"/>Owe nothing to anyone, except to love one another; for the one loving the other has fulfilled the law. <verse eid="ROM 13:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 13:9"/>For, “You shall not commit adultery,” “You shall not murder,” “You shall not steal,” “You shall not covet,” and if any other commandment, it is summed up in this word: “You shall love your neighbor as yourself.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 20:13–17; Deuteronomy 5:17–21</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Leviticus 19:18</char></note><verse eid="ROM 13:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 13:10"/>Love works no evil to the neighbor; therefore love <char style="add">is</char> the fulfillment of the law. <verse eid="ROM 13:10"/></para>

<para style="s">The Day Is Near</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 13:11"/>And do this, knowing the time, that <char style="add">it is</char> already the hour for you⁺<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for us</char></note> to be awakened out of sleep; for our salvation <char style="add">is</char> nearer now than when we came to believe. <verse eid="ROM 13:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 13:12"/>The night is advanced, and the day has drawn near; therefore let us lay aside<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL </char><char style="fq" closed="false">cast aside</char></note> the works of darkness, and let us put on the armor of light. <verse eid="ROM 13:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 13:13"/>Let us walk properly as in daytime, not in carousing and drunkenness, not in promiscuity and sensuality, not in strife and jealousy. <verse eid="ROM 13:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 13:14"/>But put on<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">clothe yourselves with</char></note> the Lord Jesus Christ and make no forethought for the desires of the flesh. <verse eid="ROM 13:14"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 13"/>

<chapter number="14" style="c" sid="ROM 14"/>

<para style="s">The Law of Liberty</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 14:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Luke 6:37–42</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 7:1–6</char></note>And receive the one being weak in the faith, not for passing judgment on reasonings.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">without passing judgment on his opinions</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 14:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 14:2"/>Indeed, one believes to eat all things, but the one being weak eats vegetables. <verse eid="ROM 14:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 14:3"/>The one eating, let him not despise the one not eating; and the one not eating, let him not judge the one eating, for God received him. <verse eid="ROM 14:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 14:4"/>Who are you, judging another’s servant? To <char style="add">his</char> own master he stands or falls. And he will be made to stand, for the Lord<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for God</char></note> is able to make him stand. <verse eid="ROM 14:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 14:5"/>For indeed, one judges a day above a day, but one judges every day <char style="add">alike</char>. Let each be fully assured in <char style="add">his</char> own mind. <verse eid="ROM 14:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 14:6"/>The one regarding the day regards <char style="add">it</char> to the Lord;<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the one who does not regard the day, to the Lord he does not regard it;</char></note> the one eating eats to the Lord, for he gives thanks to God; and the one not eating does not eat to the Lord, and he gives thanks to God. <verse eid="ROM 14:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 14:7"/>For none of us lives to himself, and none dies to himself. <verse eid="ROM 14:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 14:8"/>For both if we should live, we live to the Lord, and if we should die, we die to the Lord. Therefore both if we should live and if we should die, we are the Lord’s. <verse eid="ROM 14:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 14:9"/>For unto this Christ died<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and rose again</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> and lived again, so that He might be Lord over both the dead and the living. <verse eid="ROM 14:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 14:10"/>But why do you judge your brother? Or why also do you despise your brother? For we will all stand before the judgment seat of God.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Christ</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 14:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 14:11"/>For it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 14:11">“<char style="add">As</char> I live, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 14:11">says the Lord, </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 14:11">every knee will bow to Me, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 14:11">and every tongue will confess to God.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">will give praise to God</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">will acknowledge God</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Isaiah 45:23 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 14:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 14:12"/>So then, each of us will give account concerning himself to God.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to God</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 14:12"/></para>

<para style="s">The Law of LoveEzekiel 14:1–11</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 14:13"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 Corinthians 8:1–13</char></note>May we no longer, therefore, judge one another; but rather you⁺ decide this, not to put a stumbling block or obstacle before a brother. <verse eid="ROM 14:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 14:14"/>I know and I am persuaded in the Lord Jesus that nothing <char style="add">is</char> unclean of itself, except to him reckoning anything to be unclean, to that one <char style="add">it is</char> unclean. <verse eid="ROM 14:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 14:15"/>For if because of food your brother is grieved, no longer do you walk according to love. Do not destroy with your food that one for whom Christ died. <verse eid="ROM 14:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 14:16"/>Therefore do not let your⁺ good be spoken of as evil. <verse eid="ROM 14:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 14:17"/>For the kingdom of God is not eating and drinking, but righteousness and peace and joy in the Holy Spirit. <verse eid="ROM 14:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 14:18"/>For the one serving Christ in in this<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in these things</char></note> <char style="add">is</char> well-pleasing to God and approved by men. <verse eid="ROM 14:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 14:19"/>So then, may we pursue the <char style="add">things</char> of peace, and the <char style="add">things</char> of building up one another. <verse eid="ROM 14:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 14:20"/>Do not demolish the work of God for the sake of food. All things indeed <char style="add">are</char> clean, but <char style="add">it is</char> evil to the man eating through a stumbling block. <verse eid="ROM 14:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 14:21"/><char style="add">It is</char> good neither to eat meat nor to drink wine nor <char style="add">anything</char> in which your brother stumbles.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL, BYZ, and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">or is led into sin or is weakened</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 14:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 14:22"/>The faith that you have, have <char style="add">it</char> to yourself before God.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Keep your belief about such matters between yourself and God</char></note> Blessed <char style="add">is</char> the one not judging himself in what he approves. <verse eid="ROM 14:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 14:23"/>But the one doubting has been condemned if he eats, because <char style="add">it is</char> not of faith; and everything that <char style="add">is</char> not of faith is sin. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Some manuscripts place the text of Romans 16:25–27 here.</char></note><verse eid="ROM 14:23"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 14"/>

<chapter number="15" style="c" sid="ROM 15"/>

<para style="s">Accept One Another</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 15:1"/>Now we who <char style="add">are</char> strong ought to bear the weaknesses of the powerless and not to please ourselves. <verse eid="ROM 15:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 15:2"/>Let each of us please the neighbor unto good, for building up. <verse eid="ROM 15:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 15:3"/>For even Christ did not please Himself, but as it has been written: “The reproaches of those reproaching You fell on Me.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 69:9</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 15:4"/>For as much as<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For what</char></note> was written before was all written for our instruction, so that through endurance and through the encouragement of the Scriptures, we might have the hope. <verse eid="ROM 15:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 15:5"/>Now may the God of endurance and encouragement grant you⁺ to be of the same mind with one another, according to Christ Jesus, <verse eid="ROM 15:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 15:6"/>so that with one accord, with one mouth, you⁺ may glorify the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ. <verse eid="ROM 15:6"/></para>

<para style="s">Christ the Servant of Jews and Gentiles</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 15:7"/>Therefore receive one another as Christ also received you⁺,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> ALT, F35, TR, and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">us</char></note> to the glory of God. <verse eid="ROM 15:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 15:8"/>For<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And</char></note> I say <char style="add">that</char> Christ<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Christ Jesus</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jesus Christ</char></note> has become a servant of the circumcision for the truth of God, in order to confirm the promises to the fathers, <verse eid="ROM 15:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 15:9"/>and <char style="add">for</char> the Gentiles to glorify God for mercy, as it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 15:9">“Because of this I will praise You among the Gentiles, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 15:9">and I will sing praises to Your name.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC and F35 include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">O Lord</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> 2 Samuel 22:50; Psalm 18:49</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 15:10"/>And again it says: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 15:10">“Rejoice, O Gentiles, with His people.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 32:43</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 15:11"/>And again: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 15:11">“Praise the Lord, all the Gentiles, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 15:11">and extol Him, all the peoples.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 117:1</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 15:12"/>And again, Isaiah says: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 15:12">“There will be the root of Jesse, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 15:12">and the One arising to rule over the Gentiles; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 15:12">in Him the Gentiles will hope.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 11:10 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 15:13"/>And may the God of hope fill you⁺ with all joy and peace in believing, for you⁺ to abound in hope in the power <char style="add">of</char> the Holy Spirit. <verse eid="ROM 15:13"/></para>

<para style="s">Paul the Minister to the Gentiles</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 15:14"/>And I myself also am persuaded concerning you⁺, my brothers, that you yourselves are also full of goodness, being filled with all knowledge, and being able to admonish one another.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to admonish others</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 15:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 15:15"/>But I wrote to you⁺<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">brothers</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> more boldly in part, as reminding you⁺, because of the grace having been given to me by God <verse eid="ROM 15:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 15:16"/>for me to be a minister of Christ Jesus<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Jesus Christ</char></note> to the Gentiles, serving as a priest in the gospel of God, so that the offering of the Gentiles might become acceptable, having been sanctified in the Holy Spirit. <verse eid="ROM 15:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 15:17"/>Therefore I have ground for boasting in Christ Jesus <char style="add">in</char> the <char style="add">things</char> pertaining to God. <verse eid="ROM 15:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 15:18"/>For I will not dare to speak of anything that Christ did not work through me for the obedience of the Gentiles by word and deed, <verse eid="ROM 15:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 15:19"/>in the power of signs and wonders, in the power of the Spirit of God,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the power of the Spirit</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; NE and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the power of the Holy Spirit</char></note> so as for me, from Jerusalem and around as far as Illyricum, to have fully proclaimed the gospel of Christ, <verse eid="ROM 15:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 15:20"/>and thus striving earnestly to preach the gospel where Christ was not named, so that I might not build upon another’s foundation. <verse eid="ROM 15:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 15:21"/>But as it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="ROM 15:21">“They will see, to whom it was not announced concerning Him; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="ROM 15:21">and those who have not heard will understand.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 52:15 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 15:22"/>Therefore also I was being himdered many <char style="add">times</char> from coming to you⁺. <verse eid="ROM 15:22"/></para>

<para style="s">Paul’s Travel Plans1 Corinthians 16:5–9</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 15:23"/>But now, no longer having a place in these regions, and having a great desire for many years to come to you⁺, <verse eid="ROM 15:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="ROM 15:24"/>as I may go to Spain, I hope,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to come</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> for going through, to see you⁺ and to be sent forth there by you⁺, if first I should be filled up of you⁺ in part. <verse eid="ROM 15:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 15:25"/>But now I am going to Jerusalem, serving the saints. <verse eid="ROM 15:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 15:26"/>For Macedonia and Achaia were pleased to make some contribution for the poor of the saints who <char style="add">are</char> in Jerusalem. <verse eid="ROM 15:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 15:27"/>For they were pleased, and they are debtors of them. For if the Gentiles have shared in their spiritual <char style="add">things</char>, they ought also to minister to them in the fleshly <char style="add">things</char>. <verse eid="ROM 15:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="ROM 15:28"/>Therefore, having completed this, and having sealed this fruit to them, I will depart through you⁺ to Spain. <verse eid="ROM 15:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="ROM 15:29"/>And I know that coming to you⁺ I will come in the fullness of the blessing<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR and BYZ include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of the gospel</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> of Christ. <verse eid="ROM 15:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="ROM 15:30"/>Now I exhort you⁺, brothers, through our Lord Jesus Christ and through the love of the Spirit, to strive together with me in the prayers for me to God, <verse eid="ROM 15:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="ROM 15:31"/>so that I may be delivered from the unbelieving ones in Judea, and <char style="add">that</char> my service in Jerusalem may be acceptable to the saints, <verse eid="ROM 15:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="ROM 15:32"/>so that having come to you⁺ in joy, through the will of God I may be refreshed together with you⁺. <verse eid="ROM 15:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="ROM 15:33"/>Now the God of peace <char style="add">be</char> with all of you⁺. Amen. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Some manuscripts do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Amen.</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> One early manuscript places the text of Romans 16:25–27 here.</char></note><verse eid="ROM 15:33"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 15"/>

<chapter number="16" style="c" sid="ROM 16"/>

<para style="s">Personal Greetings and Love</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="ROM 16:1"/>Now I commend to you⁺ Phoebe our sister, being also a servant<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">deaconess</char></note> of the church in Cenchrea, <verse eid="ROM 16:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="ROM 16:2"/>so that you⁺ might receive her in the Lord worthily of the saints, and you⁺ might assist her in whatever matter she may need of you⁺. For she also has been a patroness of many, and of myself. <verse eid="ROM 16:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="ROM 16:3"/>Greet Prisca<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Prisca</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> is a variant of </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Priscilla</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Acts 18:2.</char></note> and Aquila, my fellow workers in Christ Jesus— <verse eid="ROM 16:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="ROM 16:4"/>who laid down their own neck for my life, whom not only I thank, but also all the churches of the Gentiles— <verse eid="ROM 16:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="ROM 16:5"/>and the church at their house. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 16:5">Greet Epenetus, my beloved, who is the firstfruit of Asia<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Achaia</char></note> for Christ. <verse eid="ROM 16:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="ROM 16:6"/>Greet Mary, who toiled much for you⁺.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for us</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 16:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="ROM 16:7"/>Greet Andronicus and Junia, my kinsmen and my fellow prisoners, who are notable among the apostles and who were in Christ before me. <verse eid="ROM 16:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="ROM 16:8"/>Greet Ampliatus,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Amplias</char></note> my beloved in the Lord. <verse eid="ROM 16:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="ROM 16:9"/>Greet Urbanus, our fellow worker in Christ, and my beloved Stachys. <verse eid="ROM 16:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="ROM 16:10"/>Greet Apelles, the approved in Christ. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 16:10">Greet those of the <char style="add">household</char> of Aristobulus. <verse eid="ROM 16:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="ROM 16:11"/>Greet Herodion, my kinsman. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 16:11">Greet those of the <char style="add">household</char> of Narcissus being in the Lord. <verse eid="ROM 16:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="ROM 16:12"/>Greet Tryphena and Tryphosa, those toiling in the Lord. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 16:12">Greet Persis, the beloved, who toiled much in the Lord. <verse eid="ROM 16:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="ROM 16:13"/>Greet Rufus, chosen in the Lord, and his mother and mine. <verse eid="ROM 16:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="ROM 16:14"/>Greet Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermes, Patrobas, Hermas,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Hermas, Patrobas, Hermes</char></note> and the brothers with them. <verse eid="ROM 16:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="ROM 16:15"/>Greet Philologus and Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympas and all the saints with them. <verse eid="ROM 16:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="ROM 16:16"/>Greet one another with a holy kiss. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 16:16">All the churches<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">The churches</char></note> of Christ greet you⁺. <verse eid="ROM 16:16"/></para>

<para style="s">Avoid DivisionsTitus 3:9–11</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="ROM 16:17"/>Now I exhort you⁺, brothers, to watch out for those causing divisions and obstacles contrary to the teaching that you⁺ learned, and turn away from them. <verse eid="ROM 16:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="ROM 16:18"/>For such do not serve our Lord Christ,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">our Lord Jesus Christ</char></note> but their own belly; and through smooth talk and blessing they deceive the hearts of the innocent. <verse eid="ROM 16:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="ROM 16:19"/>For your⁺ obedience has reached to all. Therefore I rejoice over you⁺. But I want you⁺ to be wise<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">indeed</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> to good, and innocent to evil. <verse eid="ROM 16:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="ROM 16:20"/>And the God of peace will crush Satan under your⁺ feet in quickness. The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NA, NE, and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of our Lord Jesus</char></note> <char style="add">be</char> with you⁺.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Scrivener TR includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Amen.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 16:20"/></para>

<para style="s">Greetings from Paul’s Fellow WorkersColossians 4:7–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="ROM 16:21"/>Timothy, my fellow worker, greets you⁺; <char style="add">so</char> also Lucius and Jason and Sosipater, my kinsmen. <verse eid="ROM 16:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="ROM 16:22"/>I, Tertius, the one having written the letter, greet you⁺ in the Lord. <verse eid="ROM 16:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="ROM 16:23"/>Gaius, the host of me and of the whole church, greets you⁺. </para>

<para style="p" vid="ROM 16:23">Erastus the steward of the city greets you⁺; <char style="add">so</char> also Quartus the brother.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL, BYZ, and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">24</char><char style="fq" closed="false">The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with all of you. Amen.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 16:23"/></para>

<para style="s">DoxologyJude 1:24–25</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="ROM 16:25"/>Now to Him being able to strengthen you⁺ according to my gospel and the proclamation of Jesus Christ, according to the revelation of the mystery having been kept silent in the times of the ages, <verse eid="ROM 16:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="ROM 16:26"/>but now having been made manifest also through the prophetic writings, according to the commandment of the eternal God having been made known to all the Gentiles unto obedience of faith<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the obedience that comes from faith</char></note>— <verse eid="ROM 16:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="ROM 16:27"/><char style="add">to</char> the only wise God <char style="add">be</char> the glory unto the ages of the ages, which <char style="add">is</char> through Jesus Christ! Amen.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Some manuscripts place the text of verses 25–27 after Romans 14:23 or after Romans 15:33.</char></note> <verse eid="ROM 16:27"/></para>

<chapter eid="ROM 16"/>

</usx>
